  'lead'         => '<p class="lead">When a commercial relationship sours, the temptation to file a criminal complaint is real: the process is cheaper than a suit and the pressure it generates is immediate. Courts, however, police that boundary. In <em>Amrit Cements Limited v. Suraj Jha</em> (C.R.R. No. 3705 of 2024, 2026:CHC-AS:1931, decided 16 November 2026), the Calcutta High Court quashed a complaint under Sections 420, 406 and 120B IPC filed by an interior decorator over unpaid final bills, holding that the dispute over work, quality and dues belonged to a civil forum and that no ingredient of cheating or criminal breach of trust was even alleged.</p>',
