$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The transaction behind the complaint</h2>
<p>The complainant, an interior decorator, was engaged to renovate the head office of the petitioner company in Calcutta against a quotation of about Rs 38.94 lakh. An advance was paid; the relationship broke down; the company engaged another contractor. The decorator raised final bills of roughly Rs 35.4 lakh and Rs 2.9 lakh, alleged they went unpaid, and filed a complaint (CS/55752/2024) before the Metropolitan Magistrate alleging cheating, criminal breach of trust and conspiracy against the company and its officers. The accused moved the High Court under Section 528 BNSS — the successor to Section 482 CrPC — to quash the proceeding.</p>

<h2>How the Court dismantled the complaint</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Disputed facts, civil forum</strong> — Whether the work was completed, its quality, and what is due on the final bills are disputed questions of fact for a civil court (para 22).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Non-payment is not a crime</strong> — Failure to pay contractual dues is a civil wrong; calling it dishonest does not make it an offence (para 23).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>No inception dishonesty, no entrustment</strong> — The complaint alleged neither deception at the start of the transaction (Section 420) nor any entrustment of property (Section 406) (para 24).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Conspiracy falls with the rest</strong> — Section 120B cannot survive once the substantive offences fail (para 25).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Abuse of process</strong> — With the ingredients absent, continuing the prosecution would abuse the court's process; the proceeding and the orders taking it forward were quashed (paras 26, 29-31).</div>
</div>

<h2>The line the judgment draws</h2>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><h3>What the complaint said</h3><p>The company dishonestly withheld payment after the work was done — so the directors cheated the contractor and misappropriated his dues, in conspiracy.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><h3>What the law requires</h3><p>Cheating needs dishonest inducement at inception; breach of trust needs entrustment. A fought-over final bill after a terminated engagement shows neither — it shows a contract dispute.</p></div>
</div>

<p>Justice Ajay Kumar Gupta's reasoning places the decision in a well-established line of Supreme Court authority holding that criminal process must not be used to pressure settlement of commercial claims — the complainant's remedy for a genuine debt lies in a civil action, where the disputes of completion and quality can actually be tried.</p>

<div class="check">
<p><strong>For businesses on the receiving end of such complaints:</strong> a quashing petition should demonstrate, from the complaint itself, the absence of the statutory ingredients — no representation at inception, no entrustment, no specific dishonest act — and frame the dispute in its true civil character, with the contract, bills and correspondence annexed. For creditors, the converse lesson: invest in the civil or arbitral remedy early; a criminal complaint that is quashed costs time, credibility and sometimes costs orders.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>Under the BNS, cheating and criminal breach of trust now live in Sections 318 and 316, and the inherent power in Section 528 BNSS. The nomenclature has changed; the boundary between civil and criminal wrongs has not.</p>
</div>
